#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222294} :activity/id #uuid "0db2ac37-787f-5ca2-b7bf-324550d93a1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222498 :tutorial/content "Each step of a numerical method produces a local truncation error $\\tau,$ which, over multiple steps, accumulates into a global truncation error $e.$ Both LTE and GTE depend on step size $\\Delta x.$\n\nThe **order** of a numerical method describes how fast these errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\nA numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the *largest positive* integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(\\frac{Δx}{k}) & ≈K{(\\frac{Δx}{k})}^{p}=\\frac{K(Δx)^{p}}{{k}^{p}}≈\\frac{e(Δx)}{{k}^{p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate with an example. Suppose a numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.21,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.21) = 0.785.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.07,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.07) = 0.010.$\n\nUsing this data, we can estimate the order of the numerical method.\n\nThe step size decreases from $0.21$ to $0.07.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.21}{0.07} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 3^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.785$ to $0.010.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.785}{0.010} = 78.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈78.5 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(78.5) \\\\ p & ≈3.97….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$\n\nBut why is order important? Well, a higher order means that the error decreases faster as $\\Delta x$ decreases. As a result, higher-order methods can achieve a target accuracy *with fewer steps*. In this way, order predicts the accuracy you gain when you make steps smaller." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e84a8b-32df-5566-99ff-2b37f338d69f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ec16a00b-18c7-517a-94d1-35859655f930" :step/math-academy-id 585132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221184 :question/id #uuid "fb9baafb-4eca-593f-ad3e-34744fdfd3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48cd517d-be8a-5dc2-91af-fbd19231363c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443240}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing GTE for Different Step Sizes"} :step/id #uuid "8b58215f-a6df-5452-b05c-85bfbc0fd9f2" :step/math-academy-id 585133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222499 :tutorial/content "An alternative, but equivalent, definition for the **order** can be made in terms of the local truncation error.\n\nA numerical method has **order** $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has **order** $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately $k^{p+1}.$\n\nBut why is the power $p+1$ for LTE but only $p$ for GTE?\n\nEach local truncation error measures the error made in *one step* of the method. If a method has order $p,$ then the error made in a single step shrinks like\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, on a fixed interval, using step size $\\Delta x$ requires about\n\n$$\nN \\approx \\dfrac1{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nsteps. The global truncation error results from *accumulating* these local errors over all steps.\n\nHeuristically, adding up $N$ local errors of size $(\\Delta x)^{p+1}$ gives\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\sim N \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} \\sim \\dfrac1{\\Delta x} \\cdot (\\Delta x)^{p+1} = (\\Delta x)^p.\n$$\n\nThus, the local truncation error is one power of $\\Delta x$ higher than the global truncation error.\n\nNext, we will apply this definition to determine the order of a given numerical method." :tutorial/id #uuid "b5d4a6f3-fc92-557f-be1f-b14832313c21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14501 :tutorial/title "Order From Local Truncation Error"} :step/id #uuid "7d7d7ca0-178c-5ff6-b4a4-b9cd8fd8f8c0" :step/math-academy-id 585136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221185 :question/id #uuid "c94bf874-2055-5ff3-83e3-b5740a1e14e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21685"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512951}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"} :step/id #uuid "c0cc635d-3c16-5296-b225-2c8ad2a20267" :step/math-academy-id 585137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221186 :question/id #uuid "b9b0118d-5238-5644-a3d6-695d8f393efe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21686"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512903}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"} :step/id #uuid "4725b391-1504-568d-8f34-bad7e7d66adf" :step/math-academy-id 585140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221187 :question/id #uuid "de3c4be6-9d3d-5dcb-b1cf-567195c4ee63" :question/math-academy-id "e-21687"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512904}] :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"} :step/id #uuid "76c4b12b-8383-5ed3-9f8c-d559ed16dcd4" :step/math-academy-id 585144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222512 :tutorial/content "We'll now show that the definition of order with respect to LTE is the same as with respect to GTE.\n\nIndeed, suppose a numerical method with increment formula $\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x$ satisfies the following:\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nOn a fixed interval of length $L,$ the number of steps is $N \\approx \\dfrac{L}{\\Delta x}.$ So,\n$N = \\mathcal O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\Delta x}\\right)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$\n\nNow, recall the relationship between GTE and LTE, given by\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N = e_{N-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nSince $y(x_{N-1}) - y_{N-1} = e_{N-1},$ it is natural to say that changing the input $y$ by $e_{N-1}$ changes $\\Phi$ by about a constant multiple of $e_{N-1}.$ So, heuristically,\n\n$$\n\\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\approx Ce_{N-1}\n$$\n\nfor some $C$ that *doesn't* depend on $\\Delta x$ (on a fixed interval). Hence, the GTE satisfies approximately\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)e_{N-1} + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nAfter $N$ steps, we get\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)^N e_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^N(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}\\tau_n.\n$$\n\nNext, note that $(1+C\\Delta x)^N \\approx e^{CN\\Delta x} \\approx e^{CL},$ which is $\\mathcal O(1)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ So, the weights $(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}$ don't change the *power* of $\\Delta x.$\n\nSo, we can treat the GTE as roughly the sum of the per-step local errors. Therefore,\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx \\sum_{n=1}^N \\tau_n\n$$\n\nWith $\\tau_n = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)$ for each $n=1,\\ldots,N,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(Δx) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =N\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,(\\frac{1}{Δx})\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,((Δx)^{p}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p$ is maximal in the LTE definition, this shows the GTE has order $p$ as well." :tutorial/id #uuid "380a28f7-f36f-50b0-982b-365f87b068bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14656 :tutorial/title "Connecting the Definitions of the Order of Numerical Methods"} :step/id #uuid "a05640d9-1389-5d1b-b299-1230e633c270" :step/math-academy-id 593404}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221478}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620} :activity/id #uuid "5f5a0a27-74df-55fb-b257-c512fcb73cb8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869} :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c42e845-2d37-5c62-8435-55cbffc5e702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} {:answer/id #uuid "336b0018-6868-5229-a732-914282396c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a27cbe-7a31-5696-93a4-64fa5ae5e5c9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d80c5e-1df7-5ab6-a247-0eedf2a21825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462287} {:answer/id #uuid "bc61ed5b-b2b1-5bf5-89e2-98973109ac8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb95d659-7aa2-5922-b590-ce7333f86a7a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb8c5c2f-7a3c-5fc5-8d79-d95df07bb8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} {:answer/id #uuid "f7308828-7ddd-5c00-8a47-b3eec31e20ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27c7694-a58b-5f51-a22b-60b4c92a4e89" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f2ba467-6ea9-5201-b847-1a519dc1ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333576" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2823fe-ac2e-533e-b8ef-d3d1dec21d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf20e996-1ea4-5bae-aecf-b59e8d34708e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a7b9303-06ef-5983-bd7d-577c4a789987" :question/math-academy-id "q-334946" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b510a71-8c9b-527d-9bcb-f5fbb3d49293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396439} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} {:answer/id #uuid "7357b661-3a8e-586f-b745-87bada056b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396441} {:answer/id #uuid "368eff5d-c023-5e5b-be1f-e7e458e23832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396442} {:answer/id #uuid "07ef22c7-7e79-5318-91fd-2a287e7cf0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} :answer-field/id #uuid "d920cb15-c78d-5c51-9a36-297e1002feed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af30b4d5-ae13-5e76-aef4-2c8e10d268d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396445} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4d56fd-96cd-5769-9c15-8ba42c5adc88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} {:answer/id #uuid "14a16a56-934d-5ec6-8e25-d2388421e668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396447} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6cdaa2-9b9d-5112-9911-3369aa53afb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396448} {:answer/id #uuid "a158f595-cfa4-5af1-86e9-3373c27d36fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} :answer-field/id #uuid "22993398-663b-5a03-abf5-707daa93f877" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e06075-4e2d-569c-b93f-165663c39144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396451} {:answer/id #uuid "f089d8dd-6f47-565b-ab6c-d6ae32100108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396452} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8aca3b-0007-5700-a38f-b489a38acf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396453} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c50b5-edcd-59fc-87f9-4fe83145da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "107.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396454} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fcccd7-bafd-5c90-975c-73cad92fbe95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455} :answer-field/id #uuid "b053a512-93f4-5783-be56-305d82bb8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b7c966-24c0-5f9e-ba19-26d61189aa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396457} {:answer/id #uuid "b09802b2-85c8-57e5-8e51-14a3469d9ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396458} {:answer/id #uuid "7a131701-833d-535b-800c-3887b7fc4a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396459} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d8158d-f371-5bb8-9048-14d8a5c82403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e74d-7332-5d6c-91a3-aaeed8c4c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5e6e5a-102a-53f8-be70-6fec86c3cf6a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481769}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513006} :activity/id #uuid "9cbd980a-784d-5a1b-93c3-51df50e05a4c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512184} :answer-field/id #uuid "290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334826" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.3125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.3125}{39.0625}=0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03959187-9418-5be5-ac79-7e4967d9b00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512109} {:answer/id #uuid "585966c4-0f5a-5998-b590-6b01a0ed43b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512301} {:answer/id #uuid "82ff2bc6-4ad2-5485-9688-6c45d0274b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512405} {:answer/id #uuid "c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512563} {:answer/id #uuid "c735b7b9-b2be-5342-be94-4a2ee43a5709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512563} :answer-field/id #uuid "01277570-5a55-57cd-b355-f92266d0d444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334747" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17647057-986e-5b9c-a8af-af759b573b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4007ccd-444f-5475-a4c0-493bff543247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e11ea327-0283-5223-89c9-1dcc1d605dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-334763" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} {:answer/id #uuid "713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512369} {:answer/id #uuid "7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512375} {:answer/id #uuid "a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512500} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512219} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :question/math-academy-id "q-334642" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424a9e92-70d2-56e9-9aed-a2fa1212a8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512238} {:answer/id #uuid "5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512308} {:answer/id #uuid "6d96eeb8-a870-5867-9c64-0e82e01c23ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512358} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c4497b-4049-5d0b-93c0-4a718cf248a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512531} {:answer/id #uuid "f6852f41-c0ac-53c8-b2e9-1ad22c6166af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512308} :answer-field/id #uuid "27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-334749" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{4}=3(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{5}=7(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512992}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512090}]}
